{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgfAWFwWraqJw2Cp1Vdmq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KfUYFuXeg93VPyc3Ugc5wz","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 1,80p satisfiability/expansion_bound.py; grep -n \"levels\\|class ExpansionBound\\|def expansion_bound\" satisfiability/expansion_bound.py | head -30","description":"Read the expansion bound's docstring and result fields"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2201,"cache_read_input_tokens":111114,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2201,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"016f94eb-fb83-436d-85e8-020ffffecefd","uuid":"5309c694-1d79-4f9f-aeb0-5b012f9b5bb3","timestamp":"2026-11-03T01:57:17.008Z","request_id":"req_011CgfAWFaCDuyPoQxAwYU8s","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KfUYFuXeg93VPyc3Ugc5wz":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && sed -n 1,80p satisfiability/expansion_bound.py; grep -n \"levels\\|class ExpansionBound\\|def expansion_bound\" satisfiability/expansion_bound.py | head -30","description":"Read the expansion bound's docstring and result fields"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01KfUYFuXeg93VPyc3Ugc5wz":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KfUYFuXeg93VPyc3Ugc5wz","type":"tool_result","content":"\"\"\"A lower bound on MOSP from neighbourhood expansion, not from degree.\n\nEvery lower bound this project had before this one is degree-based -- the\ntrivial bound, the clique bound, degeneracy, contraction degeneracy (= the arc\ncontraction bound of Yanasse, Becceneri & Soma 1999) -- and on the instances\nthat actually cost compute they all saturate near the *average degree* while the\noptimum is roughly twice it. `Random-125-125-8-5_0`: average degree 43.2, our\nbound 47, optimum 91. The strongest standard improvement in the treewidth\nliterature, the improved-graph technique LBN+ (Bodlaender, Koster & Wolle),\ngains exactly zero on those instances. The family is out of room.\n\n**The argument.** MOSP equals vertex separation plus one. Fix any ordering of\nthe customers and let `V_i` be its first `i`. The stacks open after `i` steps\nare `i - |C_i|`, where `C_i = {v in V_i : N[v] subset of V_i}` is the customers\nalready *finished*. Since `N[C_i]` lies inside `V_i`,\n\n    |N[C_i]| <= i.\n\nSo writing `f(t) = min over |C| = t of |N[C]|` -- which is non-decreasing in `t`,\nbecause any `C` of size `t+1` contains one of size `t` with a smaller closed\nneighbourhood -- no set of size `t` can sit inside a prefix of size `i` unless\n`f(t) <= i`. Hence `|C_i| <= M(i) := max{t : f(t) <= i}` and\n\n    vs(G) >= max over i of (i - M(i)),      MOSP(I) >= that + 1.\n\nThis measures how fast neighbourhoods *expand*, which is exactly the quantity\nthe degree-based bounds ignore and exactly where a dense graph is extreme.\n\n**Where the care is needed.** `f` is only computed up to some cap `T`, so\n`M(i) <= T` is proved only for `i < f(T+1)` -- and only if that `f(T+1)` is\n**exact**. An overestimate would extend the usable prefix range too far and the\nbound would exceed the optimum, which is not a slow bound but a wrong answer:\nthe search would start above the optimum and return a value whose witness\nverifies. The first draft of this made exactly that mistake and returned 122\nagainst an optimum of 91. So `f` is computed by exhaustive branch and bound, and\na level whose search is abandoned on budget is discarded entirely rather than\nused.\n\nValidated over all 6,376 certified optima at `max_t=4`: **zero violations**,\nmean gap 0.98 -> 0.61, tight on 65.3% -> 75.3%. On the 200 Chu & Stuckey\n`Random` instances, mean gap 12.76 -> 8.82 and tight on 1.5% -> 18.5%. Deeper\ncaps go much further on the hardest instances -- `Random-125-125-10-5_0` reaches\n98 against an optimum of 99, from 59 -- at a cost that grows sharply with `t`.\n\n**Prior art: not new (settled 2026-09-28).** This is the classical\nvertex-isoperimetric lower bound of Harper's method (Harper 1966, J. Combin.\nTheory 1, 385-393). Write `Phi(i) = min over |S| = i of |S| - |int S|`, the\nsmallest inner vertex boundary of an `i`-set, where `int S = {v : N[v] subset\nof S}`. The interior of `S` is exactly a `C` with `N[C] subset of S`, so\n`i - M(i) = Phi(i)` and the bound above is `vs(G) >= max_i Phi(i)`. It has\nbeen applied to pathwidth at least by Chandran & Kavitha (2006) and Lin & Lin\n(2025), both on hypercubes. What this module adds is the exact computation of\n`f` under a sound cap; no novelty is claimed for the bound itself.\nSee `literature/MISSING.md`.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\nfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n\n# Levels beyond this are not attempted: `f(t)` is a search over t-subsets and\n# the cost grows steeply, while the gain per level is already flattening.\nMAX_LEVEL = 12\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass ExpansionBound:\n    \"\"\"A bound, and the evidence behind it.\n\n    `levels` is `f(1) .. f(t_max)`, each exact. `cap` is the deepest `T` for\n    which `f(T+1)` was completed, so `max_i (i - M(i))` is proved for every\n    prefix `i < f(T+1)`; `complete` says whether the search finished within\n    budget or stopped early.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    value: int\n    cap: int\n70:class ExpansionBound:\n73:    `levels` is `f(1) .. f(t_max)`, each exact. `cap` is the deepest `T` for\n81:    levels: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)\n139:def expansion_bound(\n169:    levels: list[int] = []\n177:        levels.append(value)\n182:            limit = min(n, levels[t - 1] - 1)\n186:                    if levels[s - 1] <= i:\n192:    return ExpansionBound(best_value + 1 if best_value else 0, best_cap, levels,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"016f94eb-fb83-436d-85e8-020ffffecefd","uuid":"9660e410-4bd7-4278-b5f2-79356b3a29db","timestamp":"2026-11-03T01:57:17.019Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\"\"\"A lower bound on MOSP from neighbourhood expansion, not from degree.\n\nEvery lower bound this project had before this one is degree-based -- the\ntrivial bound, the clique bound, degeneracy, contraction degeneracy (= the arc\ncontraction bound of Yanasse, Becceneri & Soma 1999) -- and on the instances\nthat actually cost compute they all saturate near the *average degree* while the\noptimum is roughly twice it. `Random-125-125-8-5_0`: average degree 43.2, our\nbound 47, optimum 91. The strongest standard improvement in the treewidth\nliterature, the improved-graph technique LBN+ (Bodlaender, Koster & Wolle),\ngains exactly zero on those instances. The family is out of room.\n\n**The argument.** MOSP equals vertex separation plus one. Fix any ordering of\nthe customers and let `V_i` be its first `i`. The stacks open after `i` steps\nare `i - |C_i|`, where `C_i = {v in V_i : N[v] subset of V_i}` is the customers\nalready *finished*. Since `N[C_i]` lies inside `V_i`,\n\n    |N[C_i]| <= i.\n\nSo writing `f(t) = min over |C| = t of |N[C]|` -- which is non-decreasing in `t`,\nbecause any `C` of size `t+1` contains one of size `t` with a smaller closed\nneighbourhood -- no set of size `t` can sit inside a prefix of size `i` unless\n`f(t) <= i`. Hence `|C_i| <= M(i) := max{t : f(t) <= i}` and\n\n    vs(G) >= max over i of (i - M(i)),      MOSP(I) >= that + 1.\n\nThis measures how fast neighbourhoods *expand*, which is exactly the quantity\nthe degree-based bounds ignore and exactly where a dense graph is extreme.\n\n**Where the care is needed.** `f` is only computed up to some cap `T`, so\n`M(i) <= T` is proved only for `i < f(T+1)` -- and only if that `f(T+1)` is\n**exact**. An overestimate would extend the usable prefix range too far and the\nbound would exceed the optimum, which is not a slow bound but a wrong answer:\nthe search would start above the optimum and return a value whose witness\nverifies. The first draft of this made exactly that mistake and returned 122\nagainst an optimum of 91. So `f` is computed by exhaustive branch and bound, and\na level whose search is abandoned on budget is discarded entirely rather than\nused.\n\nValidated over all 6,376 certified optima at `max_t=4`: **zero violations**,\nmean gap 0.98 -> 0.61, tight on 65.3% -> 75.3%. On the 200 Chu & Stuckey\n`Random` instances, mean gap 12.76 -> 8.82 and tight on 1.5% -> 18.5%. Deeper\ncaps go much further on the hardest instances -- `Random-125-125-10-5_0` reaches\n98 against an optimum of 99, from 59 -- at a cost that grows sharply with `t`.\n\n**Prior art: not new (settled 2026-09-28).** This is the classical\nvertex-isoperimetric lower bound of Harper's method (Harper 1966, J. Combin.\nTheory 1, 385-393). Write `Phi(i) = min over |S| = i of |S| - |int S|`, the\nsmallest inner vertex boundary of an `i`-set, where `int S = {v : N[v] subset\nof S}`. The interior of `S` is exactly a `C` with `N[C] subset of S`, so\n`i - M(i) = Phi(i)` and the bound above is `vs(G) >= max_i Phi(i)`. It has\nbeen applied to pathwidth at least by Chandran & Kavitha (2006) and Lin & Lin\n(2025), both on hypercubes. What this module adds is the exact computation of\n`f` under a sound cap; no novelty is claimed for the bound itself.\nSee `literature/MISSING.md`.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\nfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n\n# Levels beyond this are not attempted: `f(t)` is a search over t-subsets and\n# the cost grows steeply, while the gain per level is already flattening.\nMAX_LEVEL = 12\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass ExpansionBound:\n    \"\"\"A bound, and the evidence behind it.\n\n    `levels` is `f(1) .. f(t_max)`, each exact. `cap` is the deepest `T` for\n    which `f(T+1)` was completed, so `max_i (i - M(i))` is proved for every\n    prefix `i < f(T+1)`; `complete` says whether the search finished within\n    budget or stopped early.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    value: int\n    cap: int\n70:class ExpansionBound:\n73:    `levels` is `f(1) .. f(t_max)`, each exact. `cap` is the deepest `T` for\n81:    levels: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)\n139:def expansion_bound(\n169:    levels: list[int] = []\n177:        levels.append(value)\n182:            limit = min(n, levels[t - 1] - 1)\n186:                    if levels[s - 1] <= i:\n192:    return ExpansionBound(best_value + 1 if best_value else 0, best_cap, levels,","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KfUYFuXeg93VPyc3Ugc5wz","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgfAWFwWraqJw2Cp1Vdmq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LKajGKRQu9xbna8sUSjodB","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 1,80p tests/test_obstruction_expansion.py; echo ----; grep -n \"exists_closedNbhd_card_le\" -A 12 lean/MOSPFormalization/EmptyPair.lean | head -40; echo ----; sed -n 1,60p reports/scratch/minor_certificates/mc_graph.py; grep -n \"def read\\|def _read\\|def load\" paper1/dataset_check.py | head","description":"Item 02 tests, the Lean theorem, the independent reader style"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2201,"cache_read_input_tokens":111114,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2201,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"016f94eb-fb83-436d-85e8-020ffffecefd","uuid":"0736d839-1d58-4511-964a-830cdb297c11","timestamp":"2026-11-03T01:57:19.264Z","request_id":"req_011CgfAWFaCDuyPoQxAwYU8s","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01LKajGKRQu9xbna8sUSjodB":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && sed -n 1,80p tests/test_obstruction_expansion.py; echo ----; grep -n \"exists_closedNbhd_card_le\" -A 12 lean/MOSPFormalization/EmptyPair.lean | head -40; echo ----; sed -n 1,60p reports/scratch/minor_certificates/mc_graph.py; grep -n \"def read\\|def _read\\|def load\" paper1/dataset_check.py | head","description":"Item 02 tests, the Lean theorem, the independent reader style"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01LKajGKRQu9xbna8sUSjodB":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LKajGKRQu9xbna8sUSjodB","type":"tool_result","content":"\"\"\"loop0013 item 02: `learning.obstruction_expansion` — the exact profile\nagainst a Python reference and against `expansion_bound`'s levels, the two\ntheorems on small graphs (the exact Harper and empty-pair values never exceed\n`vs + 1`), the ceiling between the exact bound and the optimum on the small\ncorpus instances, the empty-pair ceiling formula, label invariance of the\nsearch, the harness candidate's validity, and the committed files.\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport random\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport pytest\n\nfrom learning import obstruction_expansion as oe\n\nSCRATCH = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] / \"reports\" / \"scratch\" / \"obstruction_search\"\n\n\ndef _random_closed(rng: random.Random, n: int, p: float) -> list[int]:\n    adj = [0] * n\n    for u in range(n):\n        for v in range(u + 1, n):\n            if rng.random() < p:\n                adj[u] |= 1 << v\n                adj[v] |= 1 << u\n    return oe.closed_masks(adj)\n\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\")\ndef small_corpus():\n    \"\"\"The first 60 `targets.small` instances with matrix, optimum and witness.\"\"\"\n    if not oe.TARGETS_JSON.exists() or not oe.LEDGER_CORPUS.exists():\n        pytest.skip(\"item 01's ledger is not on disk\")\n    led = oe.ledger().set_index(\"instance_name\")\n    names = oe.target_names(\"small\")[:60]\n    inst = oe.corpus_instances()\n    out = []\n    for nm in names:\n        sol = oe.solution_record(nm)\n        out.append((nm, np.asarray(inst[nm][1].matrix, dtype=int), int(led.loc[nm, \"optimum\"]),\n                    sol[\"ordering\"] if sol else None))\n    return out\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# the exact profile\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef test_exact_profile_matches_python_reference():\n    rng = random.Random(11)\n    for _ in range(150):\n        n = rng.randint(1, 10)\n        closed = _random_closed(rng, n, rng.choice([0.15, 0.4, 0.8]))\n        prof = oe.exact_profile(closed)\n        assert prof.values == oe.exact_profile_reference(closed)\n        for t in range(1, n + 1):\n            assert prof.sets[t].bit_count() == t\n            assert oe.neighbourhood_size(closed, prof.sets[t]) == prof.values[t]\n\n\ndef test_exact_harper_and_empty_pair_never_exceed_vs_plus_one():\n    \"\"\"The two theorems (`exists_closedNbhd_card_le`,\n    `card_sub_two_mul_emptyPairIndex_sub_two_le_pathwidth`) read on the code:\n    `max_t (f(t) - t) + 1 <= vs + 1` and `n - 2 s* - 1 <= vs + 1`, on every\n    random graph tried, by brute-force vertex separation.\"\"\"\n    from learning.first_moment import vertex_separation_brute\n\n    rng = random.Random(5)\n    for _ in range(200):\n        n = rng.randint(1, 9)\n        closed = _random_closed(rng, n, rng.choice([0.2, 0.5, 0.9]))\n        adj = [c & ~(1 << v) for v, c in enumerate(closed)]\n        vs = vertex_separation_brute(adj)\n        prof = oe.exact_profile(closed)\n        harper, _ = prof.harper()\n        assert harper <= vs + 1\n        assert prof.empty_pair() <= vs + 1\n----\n135:theorem exists_closedNbhd_card_le_of_layout (σ : LinearLayout V) (j : ℕ)\n136-    (hj : j ≤ Fintype.card V) :\n137-    ∃ A : Finset V, A.card = j ∧ (closedNbhd G A).card ≤ j + vertexSepOfLayout G σ := by\n138-  rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos j with rfl | hpos\n139-  · exact ⟨∅, Finset.card_empty, by simp [closedNbhd]⟩\n140-  · obtain ⟨A, R, hAR, hA, hsum, hedge⟩ :=\n141-      exists_empty_pair_of_layout G σ (j - 1) (by omega)\n142-    refine ⟨A, by omega, ?_⟩\n143-    have := card_closedNbhd_add_card_le G A R hAR hedge\n144-    omega\n145-\n146-/-- **Harper's bound, graph form**: for every `j ≤ n` some `j`-set `A` has\n147-`|N[A]| ≤ j + pathwidth G`. Equivalently `pathwidth G ≥ min_{|A| = j} |N[A]| − j`\n--\n149:theorem exists_closedNbhd_card_le (j : ℕ) (hj : j ≤ Fintype.card V) :\n150-    ∃ A : Finset V, A.card = j ∧ (closedNbhd G A).card ≤ j + pathwidth G := by\n151-  -- the infimum defining vertex separation is attained\n152-  have hne : (Set.range (fun σ : LinearLayout V => vertexSepOfLayout G σ)).Nonempty :=\n153-    ⟨_, ⟨Fintype.equivFin V, rfl⟩⟩\n154-  obtain ⟨σ, hσ⟩ := Nat.sInf_mem hne\n155:  obtain ⟨A, hA, hN⟩ := exists_closedNbhd_card_le_of_layout G σ j hj\n156-  refine ⟨A, hA, ?_⟩\n157-  have : vertexSepOfLayout G σ = pathwidth G := by\n158-    rw [← vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth]; exact hσ\n159-  omega\n160-\n161-/-! ### The empty-pair index and the sharp bound -/\n162-\n163-/-- `s*(G) = max_A min(|A| − 1, n − |N[A]|)`: the largest `s` such that some set of\n164-at least `s + 1` vertices has at least `s` vertices outside its closed neighbourhood. -/\n165-def emptyPairIndex : ℕ :=\n166-  (Finset.univ : Finset (Finset V)).sup\n167-    (fun A => min (A.card - 1) (Fintype.card V - (closedNbhd G A).card))\n--\n182:    obtain ⟨A, hA, hN⟩ := exists_closedNbhd_card_le G ((n - k + 1) / 2) (by omega)\n183-    have := le_emptyPairIndex G A\n184-    omega\n185-\n186-/-- The survey's phrasing: an empty pair with `|A| = ⌈(n − k)/2⌉` and\n187-`|R| ≥ ⌈(n − k)/2⌉ − 1`, `k = pathwidth G`, whenever `k < n`. -/\n----\n\"\"\"Independent minor-certificate tooling (2026-10-12), written from scratch.\n\nImports nothing from the project: no networkx, no pathwidth_solver, no mosp.\nGraphs are dicts {vertex: set(neighbours)} over integer labels.\n\n  read_dgf(path)               DIMACS .dgf, vertex id i -> label i-1\n  read_mosp_customer_graph(path, fmt, index)\n                               the MOSP graph: customers as vertices, a clique per\n                               pattern. fmt \"A\" (Chu & Stuckey: first line\n                               \"patterns customers\", one row per pattern) or \"B\"\n                               (Challenge 2005: named blocks, rows = customers).\n  heuristic_sequence(G, m, rule)\n                               a contraction sequence [(keep, absorbed), ...] to m\n                               vertices: repeatedly take the min-degree vertex v\n                               (ties: smallest label) and contract it into a\n                               neighbour u chosen by `rule` (\"min-d\": least degree;\n                               \"least-c\": fewest common neighbours; ties smallest).\n  sequence_from_branch_sets(G, sets)\n                               a contraction sequence realising a partition into\n                               connected branch sets (BFS tree inside each set).\n  replay(G, seq)               replays a sequence, asserting every step contracts\n                               an existing edge of the current graph; returns the\n                               minor H (labels = surviving representatives) and the\n                               branch set of every representative.\n\"\"\"\nimport hashlib\nimport json\n\n\ndef read_dgf(path):\n    G = {}\n    for line in open(path):\n        p = line.split()\n        if not p:\n            continue\n        if p[0] == \"p\":\n            for i in range(int(p[2])):\n                G.setdefault(i, set())\n        elif p[0] == \"e\":\n            u, v = int(p[1]) - 1, int(p[2]) - 1\n            if u != v:\n                G.setdefault(u, set()).add(v)\n                G.setdefault(v, set()).add(u)\n    return G\n\n\ndef _blocks(path):\n    rows = [l.split() for l in open(path)]\n    i = 0\n    out = []\n    while i < len(rows):\n        if not rows[i]:\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        try:\n            a, b = int(rows[i][0]), int(rows[i][1])\n            assert len(rows[i]) == 2\n        except (ValueError, AssertionError, IndexError):\n            i += 1  # a name line\n            continue\n285:def read_challenge(path: Path, index):\n318:def read_source(path: Path, collect